WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:06.280
Here are seven notes, all called A, from
low on a piano to near the top.

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:10.733
In standard tuning their frequencies are
fifty-five, a hundred and

00:00:10.733 --> 00:00:15.461
ten, two hundred and twenty, four hundred
and forty, eight hundred and

00:00:15.461 --> 00:00:20.120
eighty, seventeen sixty and thirty-five
twenty vibrations per second.

00:00:20.733 --> 00:00:23.993
Mark them on an ordinary ruler of
frequency.

00:00:23.993 --> 00:00:26.700
The last step alone, from seventeen sixty

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:30.353
to thirty-five twenty, fills half the
ruler.

00:00:30.353 --> 00:00:33.453
The first four notes crowd into the first
eighth.

00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:36.847
A keyboard spaces them differently.

00:00:36.847 --> 00:00:42.467
Count every key, black or white, as one
step, called a semitone.

00:00:42.467 --> 00:00:46.370
Each A is twelve semitones above the one
before, and

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:50.427
every one of those equal climbs doubles
the frequency.

00:00:50.427 --> 00:00:53.895
To a musician, the distance between two
notes is set by

00:00:53.895 --> 00:00:57.427
the ratio of their frequencies, not by
their difference.

00:00:58.033 --> 00:00:59.813
So try this.

00:00:59.813 --> 00:01:02.563
Start at the A of four hundred and forty,

00:01:02.563 --> 00:01:06.383
and go up six semitones, half of the
twelve.

00:01:06.383 --> 00:01:08.403
What frequency do you reach?

00:01:08.403 --> 00:01:16.567
Halfway by difference would be six hundred
and sixty.

00:01:16.567 --> 00:01:21.847
Six semitones is half the climb, and two
of those half climbs must double.

00:01:21.847 --> 00:01:26.727
So each one multiplies by the same number,
the square root of two.

00:01:26.727 --> 00:01:31.447
The note is about six hundred and
twenty-two, not six hundred and sixty.

00:01:31.447 --> 00:01:34.807
Equal steps on the keyboard are equal
ratios.

00:01:35.417 --> 00:01:40.887
A ruler whose equal steps mean equal
ratios is what a logarithm builds.

00:01:40.887 --> 00:01:46.857
For centuries it let people multiply by
sliding one strip of wood along another.

00:01:46.857 --> 00:01:50.422
Push the same idea into the complex
numbers, or into

00:01:50.422 --> 00:01:54.197
the motions of a plane, and it starts to
forget things.

00:01:54.197 --> 00:01:57.867
It can lose count of how many times
something went around.

00:01:57.867 --> 00:02:02.457
It can even meet a transformation that no
steady motion produces.

00:02:02.457 --> 00:02:05.177
We will follow the ruler all the way
there.

00:02:06.383 --> 00:02:11.783
First, build that ruler for every positive
number, not just for notes.

00:02:11.783 --> 00:02:13.593
Put one at the start.

00:02:13.593 --> 00:02:17.123
Choose a length for doubling, and call it
one step.

00:02:17.123 --> 00:02:23.603
Then two sits one step along, four sits
two steps along, and eight sits three.

00:02:23.603 --> 00:02:26.143
Every doubling adds the same length.

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:30.003
The rule behind it fits in one sentence:
the

00:02:30.003 --> 00:02:33.710
position of a product is the sum of the
positions.

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:39.950
Halving must undo a doubling, so one half
sits one step before the start.

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:42.680
And since the square root of two times
itself is

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:46.590
two, the square root of two sits half a
step along.

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:49.390
Now a harder question.

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:51.280
Where does three go?

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:56.940
It lies between two and four, so somewhere
between one step and two.

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:03.417
But where, exactly?

00:03:03.417 --> 00:03:05.777
Use the rule in reverse.

00:03:05.777 --> 00:03:08.080
Twelve threes multiplied together make

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:11.717
five hundred thirty-one thousand, four
hundred and forty-one.

00:03:11.717 --> 00:03:12.837
Nineteen twos make

00:03:12.837 --> 00:03:16.757
five hundred twenty-four thousand, two
hundred and eighty-eight.

00:03:16.757 --> 00:03:19.717
Those are less than one and a half percent
apart.

00:03:19.717 --> 00:03:23.417
So twelve copies of three's length almost
match nineteen

00:03:23.417 --> 00:03:28.137
steps, and three sits close to nineteen
twelfths of a step.

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:31.790
You have seen this before, on the
keyboard.

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:38.230
Nineteen semitones above any A is a note
almost exactly three times its frequency.

00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:42.135
Closer matches between powers pin three
down further, and

00:03:42.135 --> 00:03:45.830
its position is about one point five eight
five steps.

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:48.754
As long as bigger numbers sit further
along,

00:03:48.754 --> 00:03:51.950
this one rule fixes every position on the
ruler.

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:54.750
This position has a name.

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:58.830
Measured in doublings, it is the logarithm
base two.

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:02.311
The logarithm base two of eight is three,
and

00:04:02.311 --> 00:04:05.950
of three it is about one point five eight
five.

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:10.270
Choose a different length for the first
step and you get a different base.

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:15.710
Let one step stand for multiplying by ten,
and you get the common logarithm.

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:19.430
Every one of these rulers is the same
ruler, stretched.

00:04:20.633 --> 00:04:26.253
Now make two copies of the ruler, one
fixed and one that slides along it.

00:04:26.253 --> 00:04:31.153
Mark each from one to ten, with ten one
full length along.

00:04:31.153 --> 00:04:33.593
This is the heart of a slide rule.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:38.446
To multiply two point five by three point
two, slide the

00:04:38.446 --> 00:04:42.460
moving ruler until its one sits above two
point five.

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:46.560
Everything on it is now shifted by two
point five's length.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:49.640
Find three point two on the moving ruler.

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:56.900
Which number sits directly below it?

00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:57.930
Eight.

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:01.500
The two lengths add, so the numbers
multiply.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:05.100
Two point five times three point two is
eight.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:07.422
Sliding added the positions, and the

00:05:07.422 --> 00:05:10.260
markings turned that sum back into a
product.

00:05:10.867 --> 00:05:13.507
Now try four times six.

00:05:13.507 --> 00:05:16.847
Put the one above four and look for six.

00:05:16.847 --> 00:05:18.647
It has run off the end.

00:05:18.647 --> 00:05:24.147
The answer, twenty-four, is bigger than
ten, and this ruler stops at ten.

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:26.790
So use the other end.

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:30.630
Put the moving ruler's ten above the four
instead.

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:33.830
Now six sits above two point four.

00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:39.050
The rule gives the digits, a two and a
four, but not the decimal point.

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:42.211
Two point four, twenty-four and two
hundred

00:05:42.211 --> 00:05:44.920
and forty all live on the same mark.

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:48.492
The person holding the rule keeps track:
four times

00:05:48.492 --> 00:05:52.350
six is between ten and a hundred, so it is
twenty-four.

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:58.010
Why can one mark serve both two point four
and twenty-four?

00:05:58.010 --> 00:06:02.204
Multiplying by ten moves exactly one full
ruler length,

00:06:02.204 --> 00:06:06.010
so every decade repeats the same pattern
of marks.

00:06:06.010 --> 00:06:09.150
So bend one decade into a circle.

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:12.430
One full lap of this dial multiplies by
ten.

00:06:13.033 --> 00:06:16.433
On the dial, multiplying is turning.

00:06:16.433 --> 00:06:19.353
Four sits a little past half a lap.

00:06:19.353 --> 00:06:22.533
Six adds about three quarters of a lap
more.

00:06:22.533 --> 00:06:26.083
Together they pass the top, where one, ten
and a

00:06:26.083 --> 00:06:29.633
hundred meet, and stop on two point four's
mark.

00:06:29.633 --> 00:06:34.313
The extra lap is the factor of ten the
straight ruler had no room for.

00:06:34.917 --> 00:06:38.797
The dial remembers the digits, and forgets
the laps.

00:06:38.797 --> 00:06:44.097
To read a number back from a mark, you
must also say how many laps you mean.

00:06:44.097 --> 00:06:46.957
Zero laps gives two point four.

00:06:46.957 --> 00:06:49.357
One lap gives twenty-four.

00:06:49.357 --> 00:06:52.757
Minus one lap gives zero point two four.

00:06:53.367 --> 00:06:56.867
Keep this picture of a spiral and its
shadow.

00:06:56.867 --> 00:06:59.967
A mark that can be reached in different
numbers of laps is

00:06:59.967 --> 00:07:04.107
exactly what happens to the logarithm in
the complex numbers.

00:07:04.107 --> 00:07:07.507
There, nobody folds anything by hand.

00:07:07.507 --> 00:07:10.327
The exponential does the folding by
itself.

00:07:11.533 --> 00:07:15.133
But first, the logarithm on its home
ground.

00:07:15.133 --> 00:07:17.720
The exponential function, written e to the

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:21.733
x, takes an input and returns a
multiplier.

00:07:21.733 --> 00:07:24.533
Adding inputs multiplies outputs.

00:07:24.533 --> 00:07:29.573
It equals one at zero, and its slope
everywhere equals its height.

00:07:29.573 --> 00:07:32.173
The companion video builds it from
scratch.

00:07:32.783 --> 00:07:36.083
Three facts about it decide everything.

00:07:36.083 --> 00:07:39.560
It never outputs zero, because e to the x
times

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:43.963
e to the minus x is e to the zero, which
is one.

00:07:43.963 --> 00:07:47.163
It never outputs a negative number,
because it starts at

00:07:47.163 --> 00:07:51.203
one and could only turn negative by
passing through zero.

00:07:51.203 --> 00:07:55.887
And since its slope is its height, which
is positive, it only ever

00:07:55.887 --> 00:08:01.003
climbs, from near zero on the left to as
large as you like on the right.

00:08:01.003 --> 00:08:04.543
So every positive number is reached
exactly once.

00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:09.230
That makes it reversible.

00:08:09.230 --> 00:08:12.070
The natural logarithm takes a positive
number and

00:08:12.070 --> 00:08:15.850
returns the one input that the exponential
sends there.

00:08:15.850 --> 00:08:18.890
The natural logarithm of one is zero.

00:08:18.890 --> 00:08:21.830
The natural logarithm of e is one.

00:08:21.830 --> 00:08:24.930
Zero and the negative numbers have no real
logarithm

00:08:24.930 --> 00:08:28.030
at all, because the exponential never
lands on them.

00:08:28.633 --> 00:08:31.433
The mirror image shows something else.

00:08:31.433 --> 00:08:35.173
Reflecting the graph swaps rise and run.

00:08:35.173 --> 00:08:37.600
Where the exponential has slope equal to
its

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:41.973
height, the logarithm at x has slope one
over x.

00:08:41.973 --> 00:08:45.473
At one, that slope is exactly one.

00:08:45.473 --> 00:08:52.093
So the natural logarithm of one point zero
one is almost exactly zero point zero one.

00:08:52.093 --> 00:08:55.080
A small percentage change becomes the same
small

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:58.873
step, which is why base e is called
natural.

00:08:59.483 --> 00:09:01.923
And this is the ruler again.

00:09:01.923 --> 00:09:05.140
The natural logarithm is the ruler whose
step of one

00:09:05.140 --> 00:09:09.563
multiplies by e, about two point seven one
eight.

00:09:09.563 --> 00:09:14.300
The position of a product is still the sum
of the positions: the logarithm of

00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:21.423
a times b is the logarithm of a plus the
logarithm of b, for positive a and b.

00:09:21.423 --> 00:09:24.483
Other bases are the same ruler stretched.

00:09:24.483 --> 00:09:28.245
The logarithm base two of x is the natural
logarithm

00:09:28.245 --> 00:09:31.563
of x, divided by the natural logarithm of
two.

00:09:32.167 --> 00:09:34.547
Here is the inverse at work.

00:09:34.547 --> 00:09:37.847
Money growing continuously at five percent
a year is

00:09:37.847 --> 00:09:43.427
multiplied by e to the zero point zero
five t after t years.

00:09:43.427 --> 00:09:49.500
When has it doubled?

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:52.700
Take the natural logarithm of both sides.

00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:55.720
Zero point zero five t equals the natural

00:09:55.720 --> 00:10:00.240
logarithm of two, about zero point six
nine three.

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:04.090
So t is about thirteen point nine years.

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:07.451
That is the rule of seventy for quick
estimates:

00:10:07.451 --> 00:10:10.240
divide about seventy by the percentage.

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:13.580
To grow a thousandfold takes the natural
logarithm of a

00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:18.700
thousand, six point nine one, divided by
zero point zero five.

00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:22.024
About a hundred and thirty-eight years,
close to ten

00:10:22.024 --> 00:10:25.740
doublings, since two to the tenth is just
over a thousand.

00:10:26.950 --> 00:10:29.910
Now let the numbers leave the line.

00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:36.030
A complex number is a point in the plane,
or an arrow from zero to that point.

00:10:36.030 --> 00:10:40.830
Multiplying two of them multiplies their
lengths and adds their angles.

00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:46.410
Multiplying by i keeps the length the same
and turns the arrow a quarter turn.

00:10:46.410 --> 00:10:53.800
So what is i times i?

00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:59.680
Two quarter turns make a half turn, so i
times i is minus one.

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:03.400
Lengths multiply and angles add, so a
slide

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:06.520
rule for complex numbers needs two rulers.

00:11:06.520 --> 00:11:11.520
One for length, which multiplies, so it
uses the logarithm we just built.

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:14.280
And one for angle, which simply adds.

00:11:14.883 --> 00:11:18.383
The complex exponential packs both
together.

00:11:18.383 --> 00:11:21.383
Feed it x plus i times y.

00:11:21.383 --> 00:11:25.183
The real part x sets the length, e to the
x.

00:11:25.183 --> 00:11:28.383
The imaginary part y sets the angle.

00:11:28.383 --> 00:11:33.263
For instance, take x to be the logarithm
of two, and y to be a half

00:11:33.263 --> 00:11:40.163
pi: the length is two, the angle a quarter
turn, so we land on two i.

00:11:40.767 --> 00:11:43.147
Watch what that does to a grid.

00:11:43.147 --> 00:11:45.587
On the left is the input plane.

00:11:45.587 --> 00:11:50.707
Each horizontal line keeps one angle, so
it goes to a ray from zero.

00:11:50.707 --> 00:11:54.967
Each vertical line keeps one length, so it
goes to a circle.

00:11:54.967 --> 00:12:00.387
But a vertical line is infinitely long,
and a circle closes after one lap.

00:12:00.387 --> 00:12:04.687
So each vertical line wraps around its
circle again and again.

00:12:05.300 --> 00:12:11.100
Zero and two pi times i are different
inputs, and both land on one.

00:12:11.100 --> 00:12:15.610
So do four pi times i and minus two pi
times i.

00:12:15.610 --> 00:12:18.860
Climbing two pi up the input plane carries
the output

00:12:18.860 --> 00:12:21.860
once around its circle, back to where it
started.

00:12:22.467 --> 00:12:24.187
Here is a question.

00:12:24.187 --> 00:12:31.717
Which inputs land on minus one?

00:12:31.717 --> 00:12:35.213
Minus one has length one, so the real part
of

00:12:35.213 --> 00:12:39.027
the input is the logarithm of one, which
is zero.

00:12:39.027 --> 00:12:42.217
Its angle is a half turn, pi.

00:12:42.217 --> 00:12:46.577
But pi plus any whole number of laps
points the same way.

00:12:46.577 --> 00:12:51.120
So the inputs are pi times i, three pi
times i, minus pi

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:56.367
times i, and so on: a whole column of
them, two pi apart.

00:12:56.367 --> 00:12:59.397
Every one is a logarithm of minus one.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:02.920
The same goes for every nonzero number.

00:13:02.920 --> 00:13:06.780
Its logarithms share one real part, the
logarithm of its

00:13:06.780 --> 00:13:11.680
length, and have a whole column of angles,
one for each lap.

00:13:11.680 --> 00:13:13.800
Only zero is left out.

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:19.160
Its length would have to be e to some x,
and that is never zero.

00:13:19.160 --> 00:13:22.471
In the complex numbers, the exponential is
a dial

00:13:22.471 --> 00:13:25.920
that forgets laps, just like the folded
slide rule.

00:13:27.133 --> 00:13:30.500
If a number has a whole column of
logarithms, can we

00:13:30.500 --> 00:13:34.253
at least choose one in a sensible,
continuous way?

00:13:34.253 --> 00:13:37.811
Start at one, choose the logarithm zero,
and walk

00:13:37.811 --> 00:13:41.443
the number slowly around the circle of
radius one.

00:13:41.443 --> 00:13:44.333
Keep the logarithm moving smoothly along
with it.

00:13:44.933 --> 00:13:47.713
As the number turns, its logarithm climbs

00:13:47.713 --> 00:13:51.513
straight up, because only the angle
changes.

00:13:51.513 --> 00:13:56.823
A quarter of the way round, the logarithm
is pi over two times i.

00:13:56.823 --> 00:14:01.483
Halfway round, at minus one, it is pi
times i.

00:14:01.483 --> 00:14:10.033
When the number arrives back at one, what
is the logarithm?

00:14:10.033 --> 00:14:12.453
Two pi times i.

00:14:12.453 --> 00:14:14.183
The number came back.

00:14:14.183 --> 00:14:16.303
The logarithm did not.

00:14:16.303 --> 00:14:20.443
It finished one lap higher in the column
than where it began.

00:14:20.443 --> 00:14:23.303
Go around again and it climbs again.

00:14:23.303 --> 00:14:25.873
Go the other way and it comes back down.

00:14:26.483 --> 00:14:30.583
A loop that does not go around zero
behaves differently.

00:14:30.583 --> 00:14:35.423
Here is a circle off to one side, which
zero is not inside.

00:14:35.423 --> 00:14:40.243
Walk the number once around it, carrying
the logarithm along as before.

00:14:40.243 --> 00:14:47.817
Will the logarithm come back to where it
began?

00:14:47.817 --> 00:14:52.377
It does: it drifts up and down, and
returns exactly.

00:14:52.377 --> 00:14:56.557
What matters is how many times the loop
winds around zero.

00:14:56.557 --> 00:14:59.907
That count is the winding number, and
every

00:14:59.907 --> 00:15:03.257
lap moves the logarithm by two pi times i.

00:15:03.867 --> 00:15:08.367
Could some other choice of logarithm be
continuous all the way round?

00:15:08.367 --> 00:15:14.747
Try the obvious candidate: the logarithm
whose angle stays between minus pi and pi.

00:15:14.747 --> 00:15:20.257
Walk round again, comparing it with ours,
and track the gap in whole laps.

00:15:20.257 --> 00:15:24.945
The gap sits at zero until the number
passes minus one, then jumps

00:15:24.945 --> 00:15:29.777
down a lap, because the candidate's angle
leaps from pi to minus pi.

00:15:29.777 --> 00:15:32.427
So that candidate breaks at minus one.

00:15:33.033 --> 00:15:35.260
Now suppose someone hands us a logarithm

00:15:35.260 --> 00:15:38.443
that is continuous everywhere on the
circle.

00:15:38.443 --> 00:15:41.660
At every point it differs from ours by a
whole number

00:15:41.660 --> 00:15:45.733
of laps, because both undo the same
exponential.

00:15:45.733 --> 00:15:50.923
A gap that is always a whole number of
laps, and varies continuously, can never

00:15:50.923 --> 00:15:55.913
jump from one whole number to the next, so
it stays fixed all the way round.

00:15:56.517 --> 00:15:59.257
Now follow both back to one.

00:15:59.257 --> 00:16:01.961
Theirs is a function of the point, so it

00:16:01.961 --> 00:16:04.937
returns to exactly the value it started
with.

00:16:04.937 --> 00:16:08.327
Ours finishes two pi times i higher.

00:16:08.327 --> 00:16:13.547
So the gap has changed by one lap, and we
just saw that it cannot change.

00:16:13.547 --> 00:16:15.587
That is a contradiction.

00:16:15.587 --> 00:16:20.487
No continuous logarithm exists on any loop
that winds around zero.

00:16:20.487 --> 00:16:22.117
Something has to give.

00:16:23.317 --> 00:16:27.077
What gives is the freedom to walk all the
way around.

00:16:27.077 --> 00:16:32.077
Draw a line from zero out to infinity, and
forbid crossing it.

00:16:32.077 --> 00:16:37.757
Now no loop can go around zero, and a
continuous choice becomes possible.

00:16:37.757 --> 00:16:40.037
The line is called a branch cut.

00:16:40.650 --> 00:16:44.110
The usual choice removes zero and the
negative numbers,

00:16:44.110 --> 00:16:48.320
and keeps angles strictly between minus pi
and pi.

00:16:48.320 --> 00:16:53.050
The result is the principal logarithm,
written with a capital L.

00:16:53.050 --> 00:16:56.830
On this cut plane it is a single, well
behaved function, with

00:16:56.830 --> 00:17:02.030
derivative one over z at every point, just
as on the positive numbers.

00:17:02.030 --> 00:17:07.870
For example, i has length one and a
quarter turn of angle, so Log of i

00:17:07.870 --> 00:17:14.570
is pi over two times i, and Log of minus i
is minus pi over two times i.

00:17:15.183 --> 00:17:17.743
Here is where the cut plane goes.

00:17:17.743 --> 00:17:20.783
The principal logarithm unrolls it into a
horizontal

00:17:20.783 --> 00:17:25.163
strip, between height minus pi and height
pi.

00:17:25.163 --> 00:17:27.743
Circles become vertical segments.

00:17:27.743 --> 00:17:30.623
Rays become horizontal lines.

00:17:30.623 --> 00:17:33.443
Now roll it back, and mark the two sides
of

00:17:33.443 --> 00:17:37.703
the cut: one just above it, one just
below.

00:17:37.703 --> 00:17:44.517
Where will they end up on the strip?

00:17:44.517 --> 00:17:49.987
They land on opposite edges of the strip,
the top edge and the bottom edge.

00:17:49.987 --> 00:17:52.980
That is because just above the cut the
angle is

00:17:52.980 --> 00:17:57.497
close to pi, and just below it, close to
minus pi.

00:17:57.497 --> 00:18:01.717
So a path that crosses the cut would have
to jump by two pi.

00:18:02.317 --> 00:18:05.717
The cut is a choice, not a crack.

00:18:05.717 --> 00:18:10.017
Cut along the upward imaginary axis
instead, and the angles run over

00:18:10.017 --> 00:18:15.217
a different window, from minus three
halves of pi up to a half pi.

00:18:15.217 --> 00:18:17.497
Or cut along a spiral.

00:18:17.497 --> 00:18:21.560
Any curve that runs from zero out to
infinity without crossing itself

00:18:21.560 --> 00:18:26.917
will do: in the plane that is left, no
loop can wind around zero.

00:18:26.917 --> 00:18:30.497
Zero is the one point every cut must
touch.

00:18:30.497 --> 00:18:32.477
It is called the branch point.

00:18:33.083 --> 00:18:34.923
Two cautions.

00:18:34.923 --> 00:18:37.923
On the negative numbers themselves, the
usual convention

00:18:37.923 --> 00:18:43.113
assigns the angle pi, so the principal
value exists there too.

00:18:43.113 --> 00:18:48.773
But it jumps: just below the cut, the
angles are close to minus pi.

00:18:48.773 --> 00:18:53.593
And the principal logarithm undoes the
exponential from one side only.

00:18:53.593 --> 00:18:59.163
Exponentiating the logarithm of z gives
back z for every nonzero z.

00:18:59.163 --> 00:19:03.279
But the logarithm of e to the w gives back
w only

00:19:03.279 --> 00:19:07.823
when the height of w is above minus pi and
at most pi.

00:19:07.823 --> 00:19:11.803
That is the strip, with its top edge added
by the convention.

00:19:13.017 --> 00:19:18.957
On the positive numbers, the logarithm of
a product is the sum of the logarithms.

00:19:18.957 --> 00:19:20.767
Does that survive the cut?

00:19:20.767 --> 00:19:25.437
Take a number of length one, at an angle
of three quarters of pi.

00:19:25.437 --> 00:19:26.697
Square it.

00:19:26.697 --> 00:19:31.827
The lengths multiply to one, and the
angles add to one and a half pi.

00:19:31.827 --> 00:19:33.960
But the principal logarithm only reports

00:19:33.960 --> 00:19:38.137
angles in its window, from minus pi up to
pi.

00:19:38.137 --> 00:19:45.800
So what angle will it report for the
square?

00:19:45.800 --> 00:19:50.060
Not one and a half pi: that is outside the
window.

00:19:50.060 --> 00:19:54.150
It reports the same direction as minus a
half pi.

00:19:54.150 --> 00:19:58.745
So the sum of the two logarithms is one
and a half pi times i,

00:19:58.745 --> 00:20:03.340
while the logarithm of the product is
minus a half pi times i.

00:20:03.340 --> 00:20:07.800
They differ by two pi times i, exactly one
lap.

00:20:08.400 --> 00:20:11.400
This is the slide rule's decimal point
again.

00:20:11.400 --> 00:20:15.480
When the sum of the angles runs past the
window, the chosen logarithm

00:20:15.480 --> 00:20:20.260
drops a lap, just as the slide rule's
reading dropped a factor of ten.

00:20:20.260 --> 00:20:24.200
The rule that survives is this: the
logarithm of a product

00:20:24.200 --> 00:20:28.900
is the sum of the logarithms, plus a whole
number of laps.

00:20:28.900 --> 00:20:30.940
The rule did not break.

00:20:30.940 --> 00:20:33.100
It now keeps a lap count.

00:20:33.100 --> 00:20:37.600
For our square, the count is minus one:
one lap down.

00:20:38.200 --> 00:20:41.760
That lap count hides inside a famous
puzzle.

00:20:41.760 --> 00:20:45.020
Minus one is i times i.

00:20:45.020 --> 00:20:48.420
Write i as the square root of minus one.

00:20:48.420 --> 00:20:52.960
Combine the two roots into the root of
minus one times minus one.

00:20:52.960 --> 00:20:55.940
That is the root of one, which is one.

00:20:55.940 --> 00:20:58.940
So minus one equals one.

00:20:58.940 --> 00:21:05.050
Which step broke?

00:21:05.050 --> 00:21:07.280
Combining the roots.

00:21:07.280 --> 00:21:12.140
Here a square root is e to the power of
half a logarithm.

00:21:12.140 --> 00:21:16.235
Each minus one has principal angle pi, so
the two

00:21:16.235 --> 00:21:20.330
roots together turn by pi and point at
minus one.

00:21:20.330 --> 00:21:25.800
But the product, one, has principal angle
zero, not two pi.

00:21:25.800 --> 00:21:28.380
Taking half of the lost lap leaves a half

00:21:28.380 --> 00:21:32.570
turn, and a half turn is a factor of minus
one.

00:21:32.570 --> 00:21:37.450
The root of a product equals the product
of the roots only up to that sign.

00:21:38.650 --> 00:21:40.570
One more step up.

00:21:40.570 --> 00:21:45.410
A two by two matrix is a recipe for moving
every point of the plane.

00:21:45.410 --> 00:21:50.250
It says where the two unit arrows go, and
everything else follows along.

00:21:50.250 --> 00:21:55.770
A grid shows the result: turned,
stretched, sheared, or flipped.

00:21:55.770 --> 00:21:59.168
The matrix only says where each point ends
up,

00:21:59.168 --> 00:22:02.490
not how it gets there; the travel comes
next.

00:22:03.100 --> 00:22:07.680
Matrices have an exponential too, built
from the same series.

00:22:07.680 --> 00:22:10.290
Here is the picture that matters.

00:22:10.290 --> 00:22:14.580
A matrix X can be read as a velocity: at
each point

00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:18.400
it gives an arrow saying which way that
point moves.

00:22:18.400 --> 00:22:22.320
Let every point follow its arrow for one
unit of time.

00:22:22.320 --> 00:22:25.900
Where everything ends up is the matrix e
to the X.

00:22:26.500 --> 00:22:30.120
A logarithm of a matrix A is then a
velocity that

00:22:30.120 --> 00:22:34.760
carries the plane exactly onto A in one
unit of time.

00:22:34.760 --> 00:22:40.310
So the question of whether a logarithm
exists becomes a question about motion.

00:22:40.310 --> 00:22:43.700
Is there a steady flow that ends at this
transformation?

00:22:44.300 --> 00:22:46.540
Start with a quarter turn.

00:22:46.540 --> 00:22:51.600
The velocity that turns the plane a
quarter turn per unit of time reaches it.

00:22:51.600 --> 00:22:55.760
But so does one that spins one and a
quarter turns per unit of time.

00:22:55.760 --> 00:22:58.700
So does three quarters of a turn the other
way.

00:22:58.700 --> 00:23:02.140
This is the column of complex logarithms
again.

00:23:02.140 --> 00:23:05.590
Matrices that only turn and scale behave
exactly

00:23:05.590 --> 00:23:09.260
like complex numbers, and so do their
exponentials.

00:23:10.467 --> 00:23:15.667
Now the identity, the matrix that leaves
every point where it is.

00:23:15.667 --> 00:23:18.607
Zero velocity is one logarithm.

00:23:18.607 --> 00:23:21.907
One full turn per unit of time is another.

00:23:21.907 --> 00:23:23.907
With matrices, there are more.

00:23:24.517 --> 00:23:27.397
Squash the circle into an ellipse.

00:23:27.397 --> 00:23:30.780
A flow that carries every point once
around its own ellipse

00:23:30.780 --> 00:23:35.917
in one unit of time also brings the whole
plane home exactly.

00:23:35.917 --> 00:23:39.277
Stretch or tilt the ellipse and you get
another.

00:23:39.277 --> 00:23:43.057
The identity has infinitely many real
logarithms, and

00:23:43.057 --> 00:23:46.837
not just a column of them: a whole
continuous family.

00:23:47.450 --> 00:23:50.190
Which one should a calculation return?

00:23:50.190 --> 00:23:53.270
For numbers, the principal logarithm kept
the angle

00:23:53.270 --> 00:23:58.010
within a half turn either way, between
minus pi and pi.

00:23:58.010 --> 00:24:00.370
Matrix flows turn too.

00:24:00.370 --> 00:24:03.520
Every steady flow of the plane either
turns round at a

00:24:03.520 --> 00:24:06.910
steady rate, sweeping round stretched
circles, perhaps

00:24:06.910 --> 00:24:11.570
spiralling in or out, or does not turn
round at all.

00:24:11.570 --> 00:24:15.450
The zero flow turns by nothing in its unit
of time.

00:24:15.450 --> 00:24:18.970
Each ellipse flow turns a full lap, two
pi.

00:24:19.583 --> 00:24:25.583
So use the same rule: keep the turning
strictly between minus pi and pi.

00:24:25.583 --> 00:24:29.400
Whenever a matrix sends no direction to
zero or to a negative

00:24:29.400 --> 00:24:34.583
multiple of itself, exactly one of its
logarithms obeys that rule.

00:24:34.583 --> 00:24:40.083
It is called the principal logarithm, and
for a real matrix it is real.

00:24:40.083 --> 00:24:43.923
For the identity it is the flow that does
nothing.

00:24:43.923 --> 00:24:47.163
On screen is the textbook version, which
counts the turning

00:24:47.163 --> 00:24:50.783
with the imaginary parts of what are
called eigenvalues.

00:24:58.233 --> 00:25:01.613
Can every matrix be reached by a steady
flow?

00:25:01.613 --> 00:25:05.033
Try a mirror that flips the plane left to
right.

00:25:05.033 --> 00:25:10.233
Compare a shape with a handedness, like
the letter F, before and after.

00:25:10.233 --> 00:25:13.140
The mirror turns it into its reflection,
and each

00:25:13.140 --> 00:25:17.013
dashed line only pairs a corner with where
it lands.

00:25:17.013 --> 00:25:20.060
Averaging the two matrices, the simplest
guess at a

00:25:20.060 --> 00:25:24.393
halfway point, squashes the whole plane
flat onto a line.

00:25:25.000 --> 00:25:29.160
Track the signed area of a unit square
while a flow runs.

00:25:29.160 --> 00:25:30.920
It starts at one.

00:25:30.920 --> 00:25:33.860
It changes smoothly as the plane moves.

00:25:33.860 --> 00:25:37.280
And it can never reach zero, because at
every moment the

00:25:37.280 --> 00:25:41.740
flow can be run backwards, so nothing has
been squashed flat.

00:25:41.740 --> 00:25:47.340
A smooth quantity that starts at one and
never touches zero stays positive.

00:25:47.340 --> 00:25:51.790
The mirror has signed area minus one, so
no real flow reaches

00:25:51.790 --> 00:25:55.720
it, not even one that changes its velocity
as it runs.

00:25:56.333 --> 00:26:00.253
That factor on signed area is called the
determinant.

00:26:00.253 --> 00:26:05.593
So a real matrix with a negative
determinant has no real logarithm.

00:26:05.593 --> 00:26:08.053
Is a positive determinant enough?

00:26:08.053 --> 00:26:09.373
Try this one.

00:26:09.373 --> 00:26:12.573
Double the length along one axis and flip
it.

00:26:12.573 --> 00:26:16.293
Halve the length along the other axis and
flip that too.

00:26:16.293 --> 00:26:19.353
The area is multiplied by plus one.

00:26:19.353 --> 00:26:22.773
It is even a half turn followed by a
stretch.

00:26:22.773 --> 00:26:30.150
Can a single steady flow end here?

00:26:30.150 --> 00:26:31.220
No.

00:26:31.220 --> 00:26:37.430
Suppose some steady flow X did end here,
so that A is e to the X.

00:26:37.430 --> 00:26:41.240
Then X and A commute: doing one and then
the

00:26:41.240 --> 00:26:44.490
other gives the same result in either
order.

00:26:44.490 --> 00:26:45.800
Here is why.

00:26:45.800 --> 00:26:49.467
A is a sum of powers of X: the identity,

00:26:49.467 --> 00:26:53.410
plus X, plus a half of X squared, and so
on.

00:26:53.410 --> 00:26:57.370
Multiplying X by any power of X, on either
side, gives

00:26:57.370 --> 00:27:02.310
the next power, so X slides past every
term of the sum.

00:27:02.917 --> 00:27:05.457
Now see what commuting costs.

00:27:05.457 --> 00:27:09.257
Take a vector with two nonzero parts, and
send it along

00:27:09.257 --> 00:27:15.117
both routes: first A and then X, or first
X and then A.

00:27:15.117 --> 00:27:20.537
If X has an entry off its diagonal, the
two routes end in different places.

00:27:20.537 --> 00:27:24.756
Each part of the gap is an off-diagonal
entry of X, times the

00:27:24.756 --> 00:27:29.397
difference between A's two stretches,
times one part of the vector.

00:27:29.397 --> 00:27:32.080
Those stretches differ, so the routes can
only

00:27:32.080 --> 00:27:35.637
agree when every entry off the diagonal is
zero.

00:27:36.250 --> 00:27:38.570
So X is diagonal.

00:27:38.570 --> 00:27:45.350
Its flow only stretches the two axes, each
by e raised to a real rate times the time.

00:27:45.350 --> 00:27:48.900
A real exponential is always positive, so
this flow

00:27:48.900 --> 00:27:53.970
never reverses either axis, but A reverses
both.

00:27:53.970 --> 00:27:56.940
A half turn and a stretch each have a
steady flow of

00:27:56.940 --> 00:28:00.770
their own, and running one after the other
gets here.

00:28:00.770 --> 00:28:03.610
But no single steady flow does both.

00:28:04.217 --> 00:28:07.657
Compare flipping both axes by the same
amount.

00:28:07.657 --> 00:28:12.757
That is a plain half turn, and a steady
spin reaches it easily.

00:28:12.757 --> 00:28:15.237
Why does the argument fail there?

00:28:15.237 --> 00:28:19.140
Its two stretches are equal, so nothing
forces a logarithm to

00:28:19.140 --> 00:28:24.297
keep the axes, and the spin, which turns
every axis, is allowed.

00:28:24.297 --> 00:28:29.377
In two dimensions that is the whole story:
a matrix that sends some direction

00:28:29.377 --> 00:28:33.757
to a negative multiple of itself has a
real logarithm only when it does

00:28:33.757 --> 00:28:38.937
that to every direction alike, a half turn
combined with an equal stretch.

00:28:39.550 --> 00:28:45.140
In higher dimensions, the exact rule, from
a nineteen sixty-six paper by Culver,

00:28:45.140 --> 00:28:50.660
asks that the matrix's blocks for each
negative stretch come in matching pairs.

00:28:50.660 --> 00:28:53.070
On screen is the precise version.

00:29:11.417 --> 00:29:16.137
Allow complex matrices, and the obstacle
disappears.

00:29:16.137 --> 00:29:22.237
A flip is a half turn in a complex
direction, with logarithm pi times i.

00:29:22.237 --> 00:29:26.467
Every invertible complex matrix has a
complex logarithm.

00:29:26.467 --> 00:29:28.960
Only the singular ones, which squash the
plane

00:29:28.960 --> 00:29:32.857
flat, have none, for the same reason zero
had none.

00:29:34.067 --> 00:29:36.067
Look back along the way.

00:29:36.067 --> 00:29:40.007
On the positive numbers the exponential
forgets nothing.

00:29:40.007 --> 00:29:44.317
Every output has exactly one input, and
the logarithm is a

00:29:44.317 --> 00:29:48.627
perfect ruler for ratios, the keyboard and
the slide rule.

00:29:49.233 --> 00:29:53.193
Fold the ruler into a dial, and it forgets
the decade.

00:29:53.193 --> 00:29:55.780
In the complex plane the exponential does

00:29:55.780 --> 00:29:59.393
that folding by itself, and forgets the
laps.

00:29:59.393 --> 00:30:04.433
A branch cut is a decision about laps, and
the product rule keeps a lap count.

00:30:05.033 --> 00:30:08.936
With matrices the exponential forgets even
more:

00:30:08.936 --> 00:30:12.673
whole families of flows end in the same
place.

00:30:12.673 --> 00:30:18.033
And some places cannot be reached by any
single steady real flow at all.

00:30:18.033 --> 00:30:22.273
In every setting, a logarithm asks the
same question.

00:30:22.273 --> 00:30:24.553
Which steady motion got us here?

00:30:25.167 --> 00:30:28.727
One more motion, now in three dimensions.

00:30:28.727 --> 00:30:32.920
Here is a solid cube, and a marked axis: a
rod

00:30:32.920 --> 00:30:36.287
through the centres of its top and bottom
faces.

00:30:36.287 --> 00:30:40.627
A steady spin about that rod carries every
corner round its own circle

00:30:40.627 --> 00:30:45.887
about the rod, all at the same rate, while
the rod itself stays put.

00:30:45.887 --> 00:30:50.160
Follow one corner: spinning at half a lap
per unit of time,

00:30:50.160 --> 00:30:54.507
after one unit it has gone exactly halfway
round its circle.

00:30:54.507 --> 00:30:59.727
Every other corner has too, so the whole
cube has made a half turn about the rod.

00:31:00.333 --> 00:31:02.173
Here is the puzzle.

00:31:02.173 --> 00:31:04.620
How many different steady spins end at

00:31:04.620 --> 00:31:08.773
exactly this half turn after one unit of
time?

00:31:08.773 --> 00:31:17.517
Think about the laps, the direction of
spin, and the axis.

00:31:17.517 --> 00:31:21.217
Here is the answer, one hint at a time.

00:31:21.217 --> 00:31:26.597
Laps first: one and a half laps per unit
of time also ends here,

00:31:26.597 --> 00:31:31.277
and so does two and a half, and so does
any odd number of half laps.

00:31:31.883 --> 00:31:37.963
Direction next: half a lap the other way
lands every corner in the same place.

00:31:37.963 --> 00:31:42.307
Last, the axis: a finished spin leaves
every point of its

00:31:42.307 --> 00:31:46.200
own axis where it was, and our half turn
leaves only the

00:31:46.200 --> 00:31:50.683
rod's points in place, so any other axis
is ruled out.

00:31:50.683 --> 00:31:55.803
So infinitely many steady spins end here,
all about this one rod.

00:31:56.417 --> 00:32:00.716
The two slowest, half a lap each way, turn
by exactly

00:32:00.716 --> 00:32:04.577
pi, the very edge of the principal window,
and neither

00:32:04.577 --> 00:32:09.647
is slower, so this half turn has no
principal logarithm.

00:32:09.647 --> 00:32:12.417
For the number minus one, a convention
broke the

00:32:12.417 --> 00:32:16.817
same tie; here the honest answer is the
whole family.
